  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{\u2033}+4x{y}^{\u2032}+2y=0,\\,y(0)=-2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(0)=3$\n- $4{y}^{\u2033}+y=0$\n- $x{y}^{\u2033}+2{y}^{\u2032}-y=0,\\,y(0)=-4,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(1)=-4$",
  "worked_solution": "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$).",
